<section class="paper">
  <div class="wrap file">
    <div class="photo rv">
      <span class="stamp">Rolling</span>
      <span class="lead-tag"><i>&#9654;</i> Los Angeles</span>
      <video id="leadVid" muted playsinline loop poster="/about/dj-ramirez.jpg" aria-label="DJ Ramirez on an LA street, camera in hand"></video>
      <span class="cap">SUBJECT-001 · RAMIREZ, DJ · CREATIVE DIRECTOR</span>
    </div>
    <div class="rv">
      <span class="mono" style="color:var(--red)">✳ The creed</span>
      <!-- Creed copy VERBATIM from DJ's About rework v2 (2026-07-14). Belief order
           is FINAL. "The tools are everywhere. Taste isn't." was CUT from the creed
           here on purpose - it stays the Home hero. The monuments line below is a
           deliberate bookend with the long version's closing line; both stay. -->
      <p class="creed-intro">I'm DJ Ramirez. Creative director first, director second. I tell stories, I make things, and I like being part of things bigger than myself. Give me a mountain of data and a big problem, and I'll create my way out of it. I call my work monuments, because they are a sign that something mattered enough to be remembered. Four things I believe:</p>
      <ul class="creed">
        <li class="rv">
          <b>Every great story is a <span class="serif">human story.</span></b>
          <span>It's there if you're willing to listen.</span>
        </li>
        <li class="rv">
          <b>Less agency, <span class="serif">more rock &amp; roll.</span></b>
          <span>Fewer meetings about creating, more actual creating.</span>
        </li>
        <li class="rv">
          <b>Ideas are cheap. <span class="serif">Shipped is rare.</span></b>
          <span>You're only as good as what you can ship.</span>
        </li>
        <li class="rv">
          <b>AI is a tool, <span class="serif">not a solution.</span></b>
          <span>I directed people before I directed AI.</span>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- HOW I THINK (DJ's About rework v2, 2026-07-14). Element 22 word switcher,
       same build as Turnstile's CONCEPT/DESIGN/DELIVER: auto-cycles, click a word
       to jump, pauses on hover. Copy VERBATIM from the doc. -->
  <section class="cdd wrap" id="cdd" data-i="0">
    <div class="bar">
      <span class="mono"><b>\ The thinking</b> · before anything gets made</span>
    </div>
    <div class="cdd-head" role="tablist" aria-label="Strategy, Culture, Community">
      <button class="cdd-word on" data-i="0" role="tab">Strategy<i class="cdd-bar" aria-hidden="true"></i></button>
      <button class="cdd-word" data-i="1" role="tab">Culture<i class="cdd-bar" aria-hidden="true"></i></button>
      <button class="cdd-word" data-i="2" role="tab">Community<i class="cdd-bar" aria-hidden="true"></i></button>
    </div>
    <div class="cdd-stage">
      <!-- Copy replaced VERBATIM from DJ 2026-07-14. Strategy / Culture / Community. -->
      <div class="cdd-panel on" data-i="0">
        <div class="cdd-col">
          <p>Every great campaign is built on an insight: something true that nobody's using yet. That's what I'm digging for in the mountain, the data, the research, the stuff that's true before anyone has an opinion. The brief rarely contains it, because most briefs ask for a deliverable and the insight lives a level up. So I keep asking questions until I find the one the work should actually answer.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="cdd-col">
          <p><b>The test.</b> A real insight makes everything after it easy. The audience, the idea, the work: they all fall out of it. If the idea needs a media budget to make sense, it's not an insight yet. When it's right, you can say it in a sentence and everyone in the room stops arguing.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cdd-panel" data-i="1">
        <div class="cdd-col">
          <p>I listen before I write. What the conversation values, who it already trusts, what it would actually welcome. A brand can't buy its way into culture, and faking the accent reads instantly. So I look for the overlap that's real, the place the brand honestly belongs, and build from there.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="cdd-col">
          <p><b>The line.</b> There's a difference between joining a conversation and interrupting one. The audience decides which one you did, and they decide fast. My job is making sure the work earns the room it walks into.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cdd-panel" data-i="2">
        <div class="cdd-col">
          <p>Every campaign has three parties: the brand, the talent, the audience. The brand needs relevance. The talent needs a real platform. The audience needs something worth their time. I don't consider an idea finished until all three come out ahead.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="cdd-col">
          <p><b>The math.</b> Most bad campaigns solved for one party and hoped the other two wouldn't notice. They always notice. When all three win, the work distributes itself, because everyone involved has a reason to carry it.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="receipts">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="mono rk rv">\ The receipts</span>
      <h2 class="rv">A decade, <span class="serif">itemized.</span></h2>
      <p class="rsub rv">The last decade: Sony, Intel, Adorama, an Emmy-winning campaign. I've run the room and I've taken a seat in it, whichever the work needed. Clients tend to come back. I like that stat more than the big ones.</p>
      <div class="rec-nums rv">
        <div class="rec-num"><b>4x</b><span>Agency revenue as creative director. Art of Visuals, four years.</span></div>
        <div class="rec-num"><b>2M+</b><span>Brand audience engaged.</span></div>
        <div class="rec-num"><b>3.6M+</b><span>Impressions on a single Sony launch.</span></div>
        <div class="rec-num"><b>2-4x</b><span>Engagement above benchmark across Sony campaigns.</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="rec-list rv">
        <div class="rec-row"><span class="k">Repeat clients</span><span class="v">Average client engagement: 3 to 5 years, and counting. Sony &middot; Adorama &middot; Xperia.</span></div>
        <div class="rec-row"><span class="k">Recognition</span><span class="v">An Emmy, Golds, Silvers, a Best of Show. Above-benchmark engagement, happy clients.</span></div>
        <div class="rec-row"><span class="k">Creative leadership</span><span class="v">Comfortable mentoring and leading. Managed a team of 5 and scaled up to 30: photographers, directors, art directors, designers. A team player who knows how to coach.</span></div>
        <div class="rec-row"><span class="k">Leading with passion</span><span class="v">10+ years of culture-shifting campaigns that moved the needle in Idaho's education landscape.</span></div>
        <div class="rec-row"><span class="k">Cause based</span><span class="v">10+ pro-bono projects for non-profits: victims of sex trafficking, orphanages, NGOs, and more. Building systems for ethical storytelling, for causes that need a voice.</span></div>
        <div class="rec-row"><span class="k">The roster</span><span class="v">Sony &middot; Intel &middot; Adorama &middot; MSI &middot; Blue Cross &middot; CWI.</span></div>
      </div>
      <p class="rec-foot rv">✳ Nothing here I wouldn't say in an interview.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- The long version: DJ's locked piece, verbatim (2026-07-11). -->
  <section class="lstory">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="mono lk rv">\ The long version</span>
      <div class="lbody">
        <p class="llead rv">People ask me what we do, and fifteen years in, it's somehow gotten harder to answer. The short version: I sell big ideas, then do whatever it takes to make them real. The honest version: I love telling stories, working with a great team and building things... that's it.</p>
        <div class="lcols">
        <p class="rv">We're all handed different tools. Mine was a camera. Photo and video came naturally, but I'm not technical at all, and I never cared to be. What hooked me was what the camera unlocked. It was a key to the world: access to people, places, and stories I had no business being close to. So when people ask if I'm a photographer, I say kinda. I'm a storyteller who knows how to use a camera.</p>
        <p class="rv">Where it all comes together is the marriage of two crafts: storytelling and building. Building brands, building campaigns, building experiences people actually show up for. That intersection is genuinely magical to me, and it's where I've lived for fifteen years. Honestly, I'm not good at much else. Luckily, it keeps me busy: one month it's a nonprofit in Uganda, the next it's directing commercials shaping the future of education in Idaho, sometimes it's on camera on location in New York.</p>
        <p class="rv">But here's my problem with advertising: it evaporates. Here one day, gone the next. If you make an ad about a tree falling in the forest and nobody likes it, did anyone care? I refuse to spend a career making things designed to be forgotten. What I obsess over is the other kind: shaping culture, helping people make complicated decisions about their future, giving a voice to people who can't speak up for themselves. Work that stands the test of time. Things worth remembering. That's the legacy I'm after.</p>
        <p class="rv">Somewhere along the way I noticed a pattern. On Buck the Quo I started on the agency floor and ended up a consultant. At Art of Visuals I went from making the work, to leading a team and writing strategy for clients. With Sony I started out making ads, and a year in they had me in focus groups with their product team. I've been lucky enough to be invited early to important rooms. I don't take that lightly.</p>
        </div>
        <p class="lclose rv">Monuments. <span class="serif">A sign that something mattered enough to be remembered.</span></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="wrap room">
    <div class="bar">
      <span class="mono"><b>\ In the room</b> · where I'm useful</span>
      <span class="mono" style="color:var(--dim)">Five seats</span>
    </div>
    <div class="roomrow rv">
      <span class="no"><i>\</i> 01</span>
      <h3>The pitch</h3>
      <p>Your agency has a big one on the calendar and needs senior firepower in the room. I&#39;ve pitched and won work for Sony, Adorama, MSI, Intel, ViewSonic, DW Drums, and more.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="roomrow rv">
      <span class="no"><i>\</i> 02</span>
      <h3>The empty seat</h3>
      <p>Your CD is out for a season, or the seat&#39;s open between hires. I hold the room: the standard, the notes, the client. And I hand it back better than I found it.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="roomrow rv">
      <span class="no"><i>\</i> 03</span>
      <h3>The in-house team</h3>
      <p>The team and tools are there, the work isn&#39;t landing. I plug in as a senior lead. Key info early, work in progress over polish, and notes never thrown over the fence. Giving a shit is contagious.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="roomrow rv">
      <span class="no"><i>\</i> 04</span>
      <h3>The launch with no agency</h3>
      <p>A brand with a real problem and nobody attached. I bring the strategy and the crew, first call to final delivery.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="roomrow rv">
      <span class="no"><i>\</i> 05</span>
      <h3>The problem nobody&#39;s cracked</h3>
      <p>The brief that&#39;s really a question. I dig for the insight in the data and keep asking until the work has something true to stand on.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="crew">
  <!-- DJ 2026-07-14: the pizza shot is the "less agency, more rock and roll" photo. -->
  <img loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="plx" src="/about/pizza.jpg" alt="Pizza on the stove between takes">
  <div class="grade"></div>
  <div class="cap">
    <span class="k mono">✳ The crew</span>
    <h3>Less agency, <span class="serif">more rock and roll.</span></h3>
    <p>I've been lucky enough to rock with some amazing people along the way. Some of them for a decade now. Tess keeps the whole operation on the rails, and around us is a crew that's grown with me, gig after gig, from two-person shoots to thirty-deep productions. Same band, bigger rooms.</p>
    <p>The clients stick around too. Sony came back four times. Adorama, three. One project becomes a run. That's the part I'm proudest of: anyone can book a gig, the encore is the review that matters.</p>
  </div>
